# the risc-v calling conventions: lp64, lp64f, lp64d, ilp32, ilp32f and ilp32d
#
# implemented to the risc-v elf psabi, calling convention chapter, version 1.0
# as ratified (riscv-elf-psabi-doc tag v1.0, riscv-cc.adoc). the variants differ
# in xlen, the row's word, and flen, the widest float the row passes in a float
# register, none for the soft float rows. the one classifier reads both and the
# registers from the row it is called through, so no variant has code of its own.
#
# a value is placed by the first rule that takes it:
# - a value the call passes by reference takes one word for its copy's address
# - a named float no wider than flen takes a float register
# - a named structure or array that flattens to one float, two floats, or one
#   float and one integer no wider than a word, each float no wider than flen,
#   takes those registers when all of them are free, each leaf at its own
#   offset, whatever its size
# - a scalar or aggregate wider than two words takes one word for the address
#   of its copy
# - anything else takes one or two integer registers by its size, the second
#   word going to the stack when one register is left. an unnamed value of
#   two-word alignment starts at an even register where the row pairs even,
#   as the six rows do and the e rows do not
# - with no register left it goes on the stack, at the greater of its
#   alignment and a word but never past the stack's alignment, in word slots
#   where the row packs by slot, as every risc-v row does
# results are placed as a first named argument would be, in the result
# registers, and a result that would travel by reference makes its address an
# implicit first argument. a call may be a tail call when it passes nothing on
# the stack and no result by reference, so it needs no area of the caller's
# frame past the return.

